>> A2: Briefly describe how you implemented argument parsing.  How do
>> you arrange for the elements of argv[] to be in the right order?
>> How do you avoid overflowing the stack page?

The command must end with a NUL within 4,096 bytes. Empty or all-space
commands will fail. A scan before tokenizing will count N arguments and
S string bytes, including terminators. Repeated spaces add no arguments.

Before allocating the pointer array or writing the stack, check:

    padding = (4 - S % 4) % 4
    needed  = S + padding + 4 * (N + 1) + 12
    needed <= PGSIZE

The total includes strings, alignment, argument pointers, NULL, argv,
argc, and the fake return address. It sets the argument-count limit.

Tokenize once with strtok_r(command_page, " ", &save_ptr). Copy strings
in reverse order, saving each user address in the array. Push padding,
NULL, argument addresses in reverse order, argv, argc, and a zero return
address. This leaves argv[0] pointing to the program name. Check each
stack-pointer decrement against the bottom of the allocated page.

---- RATIONALE ----

>> A3: Why does Pintos implement strtok_r() but not strtok()?

strtok() keeps its position in shared static storage, so concurrent
parsers could overwrite it. strtok_r() uses the caller's save_ptr,
letting each thread keep its own parsing position.

>> A4: In Pintos, the kernel separates commands into a executable name
>> and arguments.  In Unix-like systems, the shell does this
>> separation.  Identify at least two advantages of the Unix approach.

The shell can handle quotes, escaping, variables, and wildcards before
execution. The kernel only needs the resulting argument array.

Different shells can offer different command languages without kernel
changes. Programs can also pass argv directly without using a shell.
This keeps command syntax out of privileged kernel code.

