>> B3: Describe your code for reading and writing user data from the
>> kernel.

Checked copies will fetch the syscall number and its exact number of
32-bit arguments from f->esp. Unknown calls and invalid user addresses
will terminate the process with status -1.

The dispatcher will support all 13 required calls. String arguments will
be copied into kernel memory. Calls with return values will store them
in saved eax; halt() and exit() will not return.

For each nonempty buffer at U, first check U != 0, U < PHYS_BASE, and
size <= PHYS_BASE - U. This avoids a wrapped end address. Then check
and copy one page fragment at a time.

The proposed pagedir_user_page() will use lookup_page() once to check
PTE_P and PTE_U and return the mapped kernel alias. Copy-out will also
require PTE_W. memcpy will use that alias. String copies will check each
page and stop at NUL or the length limit.

read() and write() will check the whole buffer before I/O, then use a
page-sized heap buffer for file transfers. Hold filesys_lock only during
filesystem calls; copy user data and wait for keyboard input outside it.
For small console writes, copy the whole message and call putbuf() once,
even if the source spans two pages. Larger writes can use bounded chunks.
Descriptor zero uses input_getc(); descriptor one uses the console.

Check the descriptor first. For a valid zero-byte operation, return zero
without touching the buffer. Mappings stay stable during a syscall:
Project 2 has one thread per process and no eviction or unmapping.

>> B4: Suppose a system call causes a full page (4,096 bytes) of data
>> to be copied from user space into the kernel.  What is the least
>> and the greatest possible number of inspections of the page table
>> (e.g. calls to pagedir_get_page()) that might result?  What about
>> for a system call that only copies 2 bytes of data?  Is there room
>> for improvement in these numbers, and how much?

Count one inspection per translation-helper call. The counts below
exclude fetching the syscall number and arguments.

A single copy of 4,096 bytes needs one inspection if page-aligned, two
otherwise. Two bytes also need one or two, depending on whether they
cross a page boundary.

Our read/write plan checks the full buffer, then copies it with checks.
These two passes need two to four inspections for either size. PTE_W is
checked in the same lookup. A zero-byte buffer needs none.

Keeping the first pass's translations could reduce the count to one or
two. We will repeat the checks to avoid storing translations for a large
buffer. A single pass that checks each byte instead would need 4,096 or
two lookups.

>> B5: Briefly describe your implementation of the "wait" system call
>> and how it interacts with process termination.

process_wait(pid) will search the caller's direct-child list. A missing
record returns -1. Remove a matching record before waiting so a second
wait on the same child fails.

Keep the parent's reference while waiting on exit_done. After waking,
read exit_status, drop the reference, and return the result. The user
wait call and the initial kernel thread will use process_wait().

The child will clean up before storing its result and signaling exit_done.
An earlier signal is saved, so waiting after exit will not block. Only
the status record survives; the child's resources are freed on exit.

exit(status) stores the supplied result; exceptions use -1. Print the
full program name without arguments once as "%s: exit(%d)\n" before
freeing the name. Kernel threads and halt() print no such message.

>> B6: Any access to user program memory at a user-specified address
>> can fail due to a bad pointer value.  Such accesses must cause the
>> process to be terminated.  System calls are fraught with such
>> accesses, e.g. a "write" system call requires reading the system
>> call number from the user stack, then each of the call's three
>> arguments, then an arbitrary amount of user memory, and any of
>> these can fail at any point.  This poses a design and
>> error-handling problem: how do you best avoid obscuring the primary
>> function of code in a morass of error-handling?  Furthermore, when
>> an error is detected, how do you ensure that all temporarily
>> allocated resources (locks, buffers, etc.) are freed?  In a few
>> paragraphs, describe the strategy or strategies you adopted for
>> managing these issues.  Give an example.

Copy helpers will return failure to the caller. Syscalls will copy
arguments and filenames and check buffers before taking filesys_lock.
Each handler will have one cleanup path to release locks, free temporary
buffers, and close handles not yet added to the FD list. It will then
return an error or exit(-1) for an invalid user address.

For example, read() checks its descriptor and destination, allocates a
heap page, reads into it under filesys_lock, and unlocks before copying
to the user buffer. If that copy fails, free the page before exit(-1).
Normal returns and allocation failures also pass through cleanup.

Reject file sizes and seek positions outside signed off_t range before
conversion. For invalid descriptors, read(), write(), and filesize()
return -1, tell() returns (unsigned) -1, and close() and seek() do nothing.
Reading stdout or writing stdin returns -1; close() on 0 or 1 does
nothing. Process exit will free all installed resources as described
in B5 and B8.

---- SYNCHRONIZATION ----

>> B7: The "exec" system call returns -1 if loading the new executable
>> fails, so it cannot return before the new executable has completed
>> loading.  How does your code ensure this?  How is the load
>> success/failure status passed back to the thread that calls "exec"?

Before thread_create(), copy the command and allocate the startup and
child-status records. Initialize both semaphores to zero and give parent
and child one reference each. Add the status to the parent's child list.
If creation fails, unlink and directly free both records and the command
page; no child exists to release the reserved reference.

The child will set pid from thread_tid() before reporting loading. Under
filesys_lock, open the executable and deny writes. Report success only
after ELF loading and the full argument stack succeed. Retain the open
executable until cleanup; closing it releases its deny-write claim.

On failure, clean up the partial process before reporting load_ok=false.
In either case, write load_ok, set load_reported, then signal load_done.
The exit path will report failure if loading has not been reported yet.
The parent waits for load_done and returns the pid on success. On
failure, it removes the record, drops its reference, and returns -1.

Hold neither filesys_lock nor ref_lock while waiting. The child will
retain its status reference until sema_up(load_done) returns.

>> B8: Consider parent process P with child process C.  How do you
>> ensure proper synchronization and avoid race conditions when P
>> calls wait(C) before C exits?  After C exits?  How do you ensure
>> that all resources are freed in each case?  How about when P
>> terminates without waiting, before C exits?  After C exits?  Are
>> there any special cases?

Parent and child will each own one status reference. ref_lock will
protect the count. The last owner releases the lock, then frees the
record. Neither owner uses it after dropping its reference.

    P waits first:  P sleeps on exit_done; C cleans up and signals.
    C exits first: C leaves a stored signal for P's later wait.
    P exits first: P drops its reference; C later frees the record.
    C then P exit: P frees the record during its own cleanup.

Children will hold no parent-thread pointer and will not be adopted.
Removing the record at wait enforces one wait per direct child. B7
covers failed loads and thread creation.

Before signaling exit_done, the child will close files, release its own
child records, and destroy its address space. Clear pagedir, activate
the kernel directory, then destroy the old directory. Keep the child's
status reference until its semaphore calls return.

Never hold ref_lock during file operations, semaphore waits, or free().
Never hold filesys_lock while waiting for a child to load or exit.

---- RATIONALE ----

>> B9: Why did you choose to implement access to user memory from the
>> kernel in the way that you did?

Checking mappings before copying gives each failure a normal cleanup
path, without kernel page-fault recovery. Checking PTE_W also prevents
copy-out through a kernel alias from changing read-only user memory.

The extra page checks cost time but need no cached-translation array.
They are safe while mappings remain stable. Later VM work would need
frame pinning or similar protection around blocking I/O.

>> B10: What advantages or disadvantages can you see to your design
>> for file descriptors?

A list uses space only for open files and has no small fixed capacity.
Entries are easy to close and free; separate handles keep independent
offsets. Lookup takes linear time. A table would be faster but would
need a capacity limit or resizing.

Closed numbers will not be reused. If the wide counter exceeds INT_MAX,
open() will fail rather than wrap.

>> B11: The default tid_t to pid_t mapping is the identity mapping.
>> If you changed it, what advantages are there to your approach?

We will keep pid equal to the process thread's tid. Each process has
one thread, so no separate PID allocator is needed. Membership in the
parent's child list determines whether wait() may use that pid.
